# Current Acceleration Structure

Horse's ray-query acceleration is **not** the Rasterizer's normal visibility system. Raster visibility remains object/instance bounds + depth prepass + Hi-Z + GPU occlusion. GI and Volumetrics use a separate shared acceleration state for ray queries.

Current rigid path:

```text
scene render items
    ↓
shared AccelerationState
    ↓
unique rigid meshes ────────→ reusable mesh-local BLAS
    │                            ├── local triangles
    │                            ├── 8 triangles / leaf
    │                            └── median AABB tree
    │
    └── rigid instances ─────→ TLAS
                                 ├── world transforms
                                 ├── world AABBs
                                 ├── 4 instances / leaf
                                 └── transform-only AABB refit
```

Dynamic/skinned/deformed geometry remains on the world-space `SceneCache` fallback until a dedicated deforming-BLAS path is justified.

- `GpuNode` contains an AABB; internal nodes store two child indices and leaves own contiguous ranges.
- BLAS data is cached by mesh handle + mesh revision and reused by all rigid instances of that mesh.
- Transform-only rigid changes update instance transforms/bounds and refit the existing TLAS bottom-up; they do not rebuild mesh BLAS data or repartition the TLAS.
- Entity/instance/mesh topology changes rebuild TLAS partitioning.
- Mesh geometry changes rebuild the affected BLAS and refresh/refit instance bounds.
- GI CPU traversal is near-child-first for closest-hit rays and uses a specialized any-hit early-out path for occlusion.
- Volumetrics uploads the same shared CPU TLAS/BLAS state to SDL_GPU buffers and uses it for volumetric shadow traversal.
- GI and Volumetrics no longer maintain duplicate CPU acceleration copies.
- The experimental BVH visualizer remains a debug-only tool and must not become the Rasterizer's primary visibility mechanism.

Measured synthetic results are tracked in `BENCHMARK`. The 8-triangle BLAS leaf size is the measured winner from the 1/4/8 sweep. Binned SAH or a periodic TLAS rebuild heuristic should only be added if representative traversal measurements show that the current median trees/refits lose enough query performance to justify the added builder cost.

